{
  "text": "New approaches that use a radioactive carbon tracer allow researchers to track how tiny floating microbes take up dissolved organic molecules. By measuring uptake across many concentrations, two distinct processes become apparent and their speed can be quantified. Experiments with filtered samples and laboratory-grown bacterial and algal cultures show that bacteria possess specialized transport proteins that work efficiently when the nutrient level is very low, whereas algae rely on simple diffusion that only becomes noticeable at higher concentrations. The maximum uptake rate of the bacterial transport system reveals clues about the size and activity of bacterial communities, while a diffusion constant characterizes how quickly algae absorb the compounds. Calculated turnover times indicate that, even when algal biomass far exceeds bacterial biomass, algae take up less than a tenth of the amount that bacteria do. In natural waters, low‑level concentrations of a common sugar and a small organic acid were found, and at these levels algae absorb virtually none, whereas bacteria efficiently remove them, likely keeping the concentrations too low for algae to grow heterotrophically."
}
